{"body": "Under RSS, the actual expression is best read as an **inquiry state**: a collection of accounts plus open questions, with obstruction notes appended in ordinary prose. It is not a single RSS relation or a single story. Its RSS skeleton says: take a viewpoint, where a viewpoint is a coordinate (capacity plus logical current time) and a bag of deliveries; deliveries are evidence for stories about bookings; exact duplicates are inert; near-duplicates are conflict candidates; the snapshot is not one ledger but a collection of rival stories. The expression then carries a set of conjectures, readings, and criticisms about the frozen prose.\n\n## What the expression says\n\nIts central claim is that the frozen warehouse prose should be interpreted as an RSS inquiry state rather than as a database query or a single resolved ledger. The snapshot’s job, on this reading, is not to compute one timeless availability number but to decide which conflicts remain live. Availability is derived from a reading: capacity minus whatever reservation the reading selects. Different readings may treat expiry, releases, duplicates, and tied revisions differently. RSS does not choose among them; it represents them as accounts and open questions.\n\nThe expression then lays out ten disagreements or areas of under-specification:\n\n1. Whether uncertainty should be retained or collapsed into a single number.\n2. Whether an expired high revision still supersedes a lower active revision, or whether expiry deletes it and lets the lower one become live.\n3. What a criticism targets: a rule, a record, a booking history, the snapshot, a coordinate, or an assumption.\n4. What the source assumes about delivery completeness, duplicates, logical time, booking independence, and release/expiry interaction.\n5. Whether reservation is per booking or global, and what happens to global sums when per-booking alternatives are unresolved.\n6. Negative availability: signal of over-promising versus arithmetic error or inconsistency.\n7. Revision gaps: irrelevant under the stated supersession rule, or evidence of lost deliveries.\n8. Tie-breaking across bookings: any single availability number requires an unauthorized projection.\n9. Time: whether supersession is structural and expiry evaluative, or whether expiry changes which revision is live.\n10. What “delivered” means under replication: union, intersection, or one replica’s view.\n\nThe expression ends with an inquiry-state moral: false or incomplete proposals are not discarded; they are evidence about how people understand the system. Keeping disagreements visible is presented as the reconstruction’s honest form.\n\n## Visible source quotations and embedded translations\n\nThe explicitly marked source quotations are the five `PROSE-ESCAPE` fragments:\n\n- “the word ‘actually’ hides the problem.”\n- “possibly not even a convex one.”\n- “a partial order of possible reservations, not a set”\n- “once you move to a set of possible worlds, you have smuggled in a notion of possibility that the original bag did not contain.”\n- “if availability is allowed to be negative, then capacity is not a hard constraint, and the word ‘capacity’ may be misleading; it is more like a soft target or a reporting threshold.”\n\nThese are not RSS relations. The expression itself says RSS lacks word-level criticism, convexity, a native partial-order-of-reservations relation, a hard/soft constraint relation, and a way to make the possibility-smuggling criticism fully in its own vocabulary. So these fragments are carried as prose escapes. Any reconstruction that relies on them does not establish that RSS itself carried the content.\n\nThere are also embedded translations not marked as quotations. For example, “Exact duplicates are inert” translates or strengthens “duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority.” “Supersession is within a booking” adds a scope to “higher revision supersedes lower revisions.” “Availability may be negative” translates “Availability can be negative.” “Different accounts may adopt different readings” translates “People may disagree about whether uncertainty should be retained.” These translations are where RSS does semantic work, but their fidelity is not established by the source fragments alone.\n\n## What it leaves unresolved\n\nIt leaves unresolved whether RSS’s primitives correspond to the source’s own terms. “Story,” “bag,” “coordinate,” “viewpoint,” “reading,” “account,” and “inquiry state” may be RSS constructs rather than source concepts. The expression often says “the source says” or “the source’s reason,” but without the withheld corpus field those attributions cannot be checked.\n\nIt also leaves unresolved whether the ten disagreements are exhaustive or correctly organized. The source may not distinguish rule criticism from data criticism in the same way, may not treat replication as a merge problem, may not intend “capacity” as soft, and may not see “conflict remains live” as a positive design goal rather than a missing rule.\n\nMore specifically, it does not settle:\n\n- whether exact duplicates are truly inert or merely lack additional authority;\n- whether hold and release at the same greatest revision are alternatives or can both be in force;\n- whether an expired high revision still supersedes a lower active one;\n- whether revision gaps are meaningful;\n- whether availability alternatives have convexity or a partial-order structure;\n- whether capacity is a hard constraint or a reporting threshold;\n- how to align logical times across replicas;\n- how to merge bags—union, intersection, or single view;\n- how physical resource sharing constrains alternatives across bookings;\n- how to choose among readings and projections;\n- whether the inquiry state is a semantic interpretation or just a container for unresolved issues.\n\n## Assumptions\n\nThe expression assumes that the frozen prose is about a replicated warehouse snapshot with bookings, revisions, holds, releases, expiries, deliveries, capacity, and logical time. It assumes the snapshot can be modeled as a viewpoint plus a bag of deliveries, and that records are evidence for stories rather than facts.\n\nIt assumes conflict is material, not error. It assumes exact duplication adds no authority. It assumes supersession is structural within a booking, while across bookings it has no force. It assumes expiry and release policies are readings, not snapshot facts. It assumes availability can be negative and that the sign is a signal rather than an arithmetic error. It assumes accounts are not merged and disagreement is first-class. It assumes an inquiry state can carry conjectures, commitments, refutations, and open questions. Finally, it assumes that noncomputability is acceptable for this semantic class—not a defect to be fixed by an algorithm.\n\n## Criticisms and competing readings\n\nOne criticism is that RSS imposes its own ontology on the source. By treating the snapshot as rival stories, accounts, readings, and possible reservations, RSS may smuggle in exactly the kind of possibility vocabulary that the quoted source criticism warns against. The expression admits this circularity: RSS can register the criticism, but cannot make it fully in RSS vocabulary without already using the vocabulary the criticism targets.\n\nAnother criticism is operational. RSS refuses to produce a single answer, lacks a common metric for comparing accounts, requires external choice, and does not include a resource model unless one is added. It may obstruct decision-making, automation, resource reasoning, time alignment, and the very idea of a snapshot as a single object. The expression itself lists these as obstructions.\n\nA third criticism is that the inquiry state may be too permissive. If every unresolved issue can be carried as an open question, RSS can preserve disagreement without resolving meaning. That is valuable for honesty, but a critic can say it is a filing system rather than a semantics. The final claim that keeping disagreements visible is “the reconstruction’s only honest form” is normative, not forced. A competing reading says that sometimes the honest form is to adopt a projection and justify it.\n\nCompeting readings of the source include:\n\n- **Descriptive versus normative conflict**: “Which conflicts remain live” could be a description of what the snapshot happens not to resolve, or a normative requirement that it should not resolve them.\n- **Structural versus evaluative supersession**: a high revision may always win but reserve zero after expiry, or expiry may remove it and let a lower revision become live.\n- **Hard versus soft capacity**: negative availability may signal over-reservation, or it may show that “capacity” is only a reporting threshold.\n- **Union, intersection, or single-view replication**: the snapshot may contain alternatives no single replica has, may omit records that matter, or may just be one replica’s view.\n- **Inquiry as success versus inquiry as evasion**: preserving open questions may be the only honest reconstruction, or it may push the real problem downstream.\n\nIn short, the expression is an RSS account that classifies the frozen prose into stories, readings, conjectures, disagreements, and obstruction notes. It does not prove that RSS carries the source’s full content. The explicitly escaped source quotations remain outside RSS proper, and the expression’s own obstruction notes confirm that several substantive criticisms—convexity, partial order, possibility smuggling, hard/soft capacity, and word-level hiding—are not carried by RSS relations.", "commitments": "Under RSS, the actual expression is best read as an **inquiry state**: a collection of accounts plus open questions, with obstruction notes appended in ordinary prose. It is not a single RSS relation or a single story. Its RSS skeleton says: take a viewpoint, where a viewpoint is a coordinate (capacity plus logical current t